Chapter: Pipeline

  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Shell chapter ShellTypes

 A build is a graph of STAGES, and a stage is an input, a transformation and an
 output. What ships today is a flat list of shell statements per script, so a
 stage exists only as a section header and a variable name, and nothing can be
 asked what a step consumes, what a step produces, or what a failure of the step
 means. The three emitters beside this chapter answer "how do I say this in
 PowerShell"; nothing answers "what is the build doing".

 THE MODEL IS A CHAPTER OF ITS OWN AND NOT NEW CONSTRUCTORS IN `ShellTypes`,
 and the reason is measured rather than tidy. `ShellExpr` gaining ONE
 constructor cost `cdxtopeScript` 4.75 MB of check deck and put three units on
 the 1.25 floor at once, because every generator bundles the type and a new
 constructor widens the checker's state at every `when` over it
 (ShellDslReadability.md section 9). A separate chapter is paid for by the
 generators that cite it and by nobody else, which is Library Rule 7: a
 foreword chapter nobody cites is never in the unit at all.

 WHAT MAKES A DECLARATION MORE THAN A COMMENT is that something reads it, and
 the complaint this model answers is precisely that the current stage names ARE
 comments. So a stage body does not spell `SeVar "srcPath"`; it asks the
 pipeline for the artifact named `srcPath`, and `pipe-ref` answers from the
 declarations. Delete the declaration and the reference resolves to
 `UNRESOLVED:srcPath`, which changes the emitted script, which reds
 `check-generated-scripts`. The declarations are load-bearing in the direction
 that matters first: a name used and not declared cannot pass.

 WHAT IS DECLARED AND NOT YET LOAD-BEARING, said plainly rather than implied:
 resources and verdicts. A verdict that reads "exit 3 means a cited chapter
 could not be resolved" sits beside an `ScExit (SeInt 3)` inside a `try` arm
 that a generic lowering cannot place, and placing it needs the named-operation
 vocabulary that is the next pass. A declared artifact that no body references
 is likewise not yet caught. Both are the second pass, and until then a reader
 of this chapter should treat those two fields as documentation with a schema.

Section: Artifacts

 An artifact is a thing a stage consumes or produces, named so another stage
 can refer to it, and carrying the expression the target language uses to reach
 it. The kind is what a non-shell target needs in order to lower the same model
 differently: a file is a path to a CI runner and a variable to a shell.

  PipeKind =
    | PkFile
    | PkDir
    | PkValue
    | PkText

 AN ARTIFACT HAS TWO EXPRESSIONS AND CONFLATING THEM IS THE SECOND THING THIS
 chapter got wrong. `Repo` is PRODUCED by `(Resolve-Path (Join-Path
 $PSScriptRoot '..')).Path` and REFERRED TO as `$Repo`, and a model carrying one
 field emitted the production at every use site: the first migration came out
 one line from byte-identical, with the whole path computation inlined into a
 call argument. `pa-expr` is how a consumer names the artifact; `pa-from` is how
 the producing stage makes it.

 `pa-from` IS A `Maybe` BECAUSE NOT EVERY ARTIFACT IS A PLAIN ASSIGNMENT.
 `rootLines` comes from a .NET call statement rather than an expression
 assignment, and a model that pretended otherwise would carry a value nothing
 emits. `None` says the producing stage spells its own statement, and says it in
 the data rather than in a comment.

 A PRODUCING EXPRESSION MUST NOT REACH THE ARTIFACT TABLE. The table contains
 the artifact, so an expression inside the artifact that resolves through the
 table is the same circular definition that faulted the first shape. A
 production that needs a parameter uses `pipe-param-ref`, which reads the
 parameter list alone; a production that needs another stage's output is `None`
 and is spelled in the body.

  PipeArtifact = record {
    pa-name : Text,
    pa-kind : PipeKind,
    pa-expr : ShellExpr,
    pa-from : Maybe ShellExpr
  }

Section: Resources

 What the stage needs from the box. Nothing reads these yet; they are declared
 because the box is the scarcest thing the fleet has and a stage that boots four
 guests should say so where a scheduler can see it rather than in a comment.

  PipeResource =
    | PrGuests (Integer)
    | PrMemoryMb (Integer)
    | PrPort (Integer)
    | PrKernel (ShellExpr)

Section: Verdicts

 What an exit code MEANS. A script today spells the number and leaves the
 meaning to a comment, so a reader has to guess whether 3 is a refusal or a
 crash.

 `PoPassthrough` EXISTS BECAUSE A LITERAL CODE CANNOT SAY WHAT `run-plug-chain`
 DOES, which is exit with the code of the child it ran. The second migration
 found the gap: without this constructor the one interesting exit of that
 script has no declaration at all, and a verdict list that omits the exit a
 reader most wants explained is worse than no verdict list.

  PipeOutcome =
    | PoExit (Integer) (Text)
    | PoPassthrough (Text)

Section: Stages

  PipeStage = record {
    ps-name : Text,
    ps-why : Text,
    ps-in : List Text,
    ps-out : List PipeArtifact,
    ps-needs : List PipeResource,
    ps-verdict : List PipeOutcome,
    ps-body : List ShellCmd
  }

  Pipeline = record {
    pl-name : Text,
    pl-doc : Text,
    pl-params : List ShellParam,
    pl-stages : List PipeStage
  }

Section: Resolving a name

 Parameters are artifacts too, and a parameter is reached by its own name in
 every one of the three shells, so a parameter resolves to `SeVar` of its name
 without being declared twice.

  pipe-param-named : List ShellParam, Text, Integer -> Boolean
  pipe-param-named (ps) (name) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then False
    else if ((list-at ps i).sp-name) == name then True
    else pipe-param-named ps name (i + 1)

  pipe-find-in-outs : List PipeArtifact, Text, Integer -> Maybe ShellExpr
  pipe-find-in-outs (outs) (name) (i) =
    if i >= list-length outs then None
    else let a = list-at outs i
    in if (a.pa-name) == name then Just (a.pa-expr)
    else pipe-find-in-outs outs name (i + 1)

 RESOLUTION IS AGAINST A DECLARED TABLE, NEVER AGAINST THE ASSEMBLED PIPELINE,
 and the first shape of this chapter got that wrong in a way worth keeping
 written down. `pipe-ref` originally took the `Pipeline`, so a stage body asked
 the pipeline for a name, and the pipeline was built FROM the stages: the
 generator compiled clean and faulted at run with `!EXC=08`, a stack overflow
 from a definition that contains itself. The table breaks the cycle, and the
 artifacts stay load-bearing because the table and each stage's `ps-out` hold
 THE SAME named definitions, so deleting an artifact is a compile error rather
 than a silent drift.

 An unresolved name is spelled into the emitted script rather than swallowed.
 The drift check is the gate this model migrates under, so a reference to a name
 nobody declares has to CHANGE THE OUTPUT to be caught, and a silent fallback to
 `SeVar name` would emit a script that looks right and reds nothing.

  pipe-ref : List PipeArtifact, List ShellParam, Text -> ShellExpr
  pipe-ref (arts) (params) (name) =
    if pipe-param-named params name 0 then SeVar name
    else when pipe-find-in-outs arts name 0
      is Just (e) -> e
      is None -> SeLit ("UNRESOLVED:" & name)

 The parameter-only resolver, for an expression that lives INSIDE an artifact
 and therefore cannot read the table.

  pipe-param-ref : List ShellParam, Text -> ShellExpr
  pipe-param-ref (params) (name) =
    if pipe-param-named params name 0 then SeVar name
    else SeLit ("UNRESOLVED-PARAM:" & name)

 The producing statement comes from the declaration rather than from the body,
 so an artifact and the line that makes it cannot disagree. An artifact that
 declares no production and is asked for one says so in the emitted text, which
 the drift check reads.

  pipe-produce : PipeArtifact -> ShellCmd
  pipe-produce (a) =
    when a.pa-from
      is Just (e) -> ScAssign (a.pa-name) e
      is None -> ScComment ("UNPRODUCED:" & (a.pa-name))

Section: Lowering to the shell AST

 The first lowering is a concatenation, and the blank-line placement stays
 inside the stage bodies where the generators already carry it. That is what
 makes the first migration byte-identical: the model adds declarations and
 changes no emitted character. A lowering that also OWNED the spacing would be
 a better model and a worse migration, and the migration is the gate.

 EACH STAGE LOWERS AS ITS OWN `ScSequence` RATHER THAN BEING CONCATENATED FLAT,
 and the difference is a blank line per stage boundary: `emit-ps-cmds` gives a
 nested sequence a trailing newline that a flat list does not get. Concatenating
 the bodies emitted a script three blank lines shorter than the one that ships,
 which is the drift check earning its place on a change that touched no
 statement.

  pipe-stage-cmds : List PipeStage, Integer, List ShellCmd -> List ShellCmd
  pipe-stage-cmds (ss) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ss then acc
    else pipe-stage-cmds ss (i + 1) (list-push acc (ScSequence ((list-at ss i).ps-body)))

  pipeline-to-shell : Pipeline -> ShellScript
  pipeline-to-shell (pl) =
    ShellScript {
      ss-name = pl.pl-name,
      ss-description = pl.pl-doc,
      ss-params = pl.pl-params,
      ss-body = pipe-stage-cmds (pl.pl-stages) 0 []
    }

Section: Reading the model back

 The stage list rendered as text, which is the coverage metric the campaign
 moves to and the answer to "what is the build doing" that no generator can
 give today.

  pipe-kind-text : PipeKind -> Text
  pipe-kind-text (k) =
    when k
      is PkFile -> "file"
      is PkDir -> "dir"
      is PkValue -> "value"
      is PkText -> "text"

  pipe-arts-text : List PipeArtifact, Integer, Text -> Text
  pipe-arts-text (as) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length as then acc
    else let a = list-at as i
    in pipe-arts-text as (i + 1) (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else ", ") & (a.pa-name) & ":" & pipe-kind-text (a.pa-kind))

  pipe-names-text : List Text, Integer, Text -> Text
  pipe-names-text (ns) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ns then acc
    else pipe-names-text ns (i + 1) (acc & (if acc == "" then "" else ", ") & list-at ns i)

  pipe-stage-line : PipeStage -> Text
  pipe-stage-line (s) =
    (s.ps-name) & " | in: " & pipe-names-text (s.ps-in) 0 ""
      & " | out: " & pipe-arts-text (s.ps-out) 0 ""
      & " | " & (s.ps-why)

  pipe-doc-lines : List PipeStage, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  pipe-doc-lines (ss) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ss then acc
    else pipe-doc-lines ss (i + 1) (list-push acc (pipe-stage-line (list-at ss i)))

  pipeline-doc : Pipeline -> List Text
  pipeline-doc (pl) = pipe-doc-lines (pl.pl-stages) 0 []

